<p>There’s a decent chance you’ve heard at least a couple of tracks from Wendy Carlos’ <em><a href="https://www.openculture.com/2018/10/wendy-carlos-switched-on-bach-turns-50.html">Switched-On Bach</a></em>. But when was the last time you put on <em>Switched-On Rock</em>, <em>Switched-On Country</em>, <em>Switched-On Santa</em>, or <em>Switched-On Bacharach</em>? More likely than not, you’ve never even heard of those other albums, which have nothing to do with Carlos and everything to do with the music-industry fad that her then-high-tech renditions of Bach set off in the late nineteen-sixties and early seventies. That’s the subject of <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XPaYtGIyRvg">the new Bandsplaining video above</a>,&nbsp;which covers the “awkward and glorious” releases that constituted the initial waves of electronic pop music.</p>
<p>Like so many cultural shifts, the rise of electronic pop took place thanks to a technological change. To be more specific, it was made possible by the invention of the voltage-controlled, modular synthesizer, itself dependent on two particular innovations. Voltage control permitted the pitch of the sounds being generated to be selected with a piano-style keyboard, a slide ribbon, a sequencer, or any other interface the creator’s electronics know-how would allow. In the context of synthesizers, a module is “a little box with an input and an output that did one thing and one thing only, either making a waveform or modifying it somehow,” as Robert Moog once described it, adding that multiple modules could be connected together with cables.</p>
