<p>If the name Moog sounds familiar, that’s because the lines of synthesizers that bear it <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2016/02/how-the-moog-synthesizer-changed-the-sound-of-music.html">changed music forever</a>. In fact, Carlos used&nbsp;an early Moog model to record <em>Switched-On Bach</em>, having custom-built it in collaboration with the inventor himself. Though the machine set up behind the Bach impersonator on that album’s cover may look unwieldy today, it represented a major improvement in convenience over the room-filling, musically unintuitive behemoths previously accessible only at certain universities and corporate R&amp;D departments. So did the synthesizer invented by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Buchla">Don Buchla</a> on the west coast, at about the same time Moog was building his on the east coast, though each man’s design expressed a different vision of how electronic instruments would adapt to music, or vice versa.</p>
<p>Indeed, up through at least the first few years of the seventies, it would’ve been impossible to say how or whether electronic sounds would find their place in mainstream records. The emergence of home hi-fi culture created a market for swinging “space age pop” by the likes of Les Baxter, Dick Hyman, and Juan Garcia Esquivel, the subject of <a href="https://youtu.be/wEjWpbtOxAM">Bandsplaining’s previous video</a>. This one highlights such releases as Richard Hayman’s <em>Genuine Electric Latin Love Machine </em>and Gershon Kingsley’s <em>Music to Moog By</em>,&nbsp;whose track <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OSRCemf2JHc">“Popcorn”</a> would become a worldwide hit&nbsp;when <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uS3_SgILyy8">re-recorded by Hot Butter</a> in 1972, but not before the space-age composer Hyman got in on the action. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QgJNs25qsws">“The Minotaur,”</a> his piece of hypnotic psychedelia with an Indian drone and Greek melody, reached #38 on the Hot 100 57 years ago — and today, remains a thrilling eight-and-a-half-minute ride down one of the many roads electronic pop music didn’t take.</p>
